.TH RADIOCAL 1
.SH NAME
radiocal, radiocfg \- calibrate a radio, and apply what was measured
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiocal
.B -t
[
.B -n
.I per-rung
] [
.B -s
.I step
] [
.B -f
.I first
] [
.B -l
.I last
]
.PP
.B radiocal
.B -r
[
.B -w
.I seconds
]
.PP
.B radiocfg
[
.B -n
] [
.B -s
] [
.B -f
.I file
] [
.B -u
.I uid
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Radiocal
measures what a decibel of transmitter is worth to a receiver, using the
transmitter's own power amplifier as the attenuator.  It is two halves of
one program, run on two boards.
.PP
.B -t
walks
.BR PA_CFG2 's
.B PA_POWER_RAMP
field from
.I first
down to
.I last
in steps of
.IR step ,
sending
.I per-rung
packets at each.  SWRU295E gives the field's output as
.BR "(PA_POWER_RAMP + 1)/2 \- 18" dBm,
so one count is half a decibel over a 31.5dB range.  Each value is
written through the radio's
.B ctl
and
.B "read back off the part"
before anything is sent: a rung whose register did not take is not a
rung, and the run stops rather than reporting it.
.PP
Every packet carries the rung and a sequence number.  That is the whole
difference between this and driving the same ladder from a host: the
transmitter's step and the receiver's reading are joined by the packet
and not by whatever is typing at the two consoles, so a dropped console
line cannot attribute a reading to the wrong rung.
.PP
.B -r
holds
.B #W/radio0/pkt
open for
.I seconds
and tabulates, per rung, how many of the sequence arrived, how many are
missing, how many had a CRC error, and the mean, least and greatest
RSSI.  It then prints the least-squares slope of RSSI against the
datasheet's dBm.
.PP
It uses
.B pkt
and not
.B data
because
.I radio (3)
hands over a twelve-byte header with every packet \- length, RSSI, SNR,
flags and arrival tick, out of the same queue entry \- so a rung gets a
spread and a per-packet CRC flag.  The stat file's
.B last
gives one sample however many packets a rung sent, and the spread is one
decibel, which that cannot show.
.SS "What the slope is and is not"
It is the
.B product
of two things: how accurately
.B PA_POWER_RAMP
steps, and how accurately this receiver's RSSI reports a change.  A slope
of one means both are right or both are wrong by reciprocal amounts, and
nothing here separates them.  The program prints that with its answer,
because a slope without it is a number somebody will quote.
.PP
It gives
.B no offset.
Free space between two antennas is an unknown loss, and the offset wants
a calibrated generator cabled into the click's u.FL.
.PP
Three independent ladders have now been measured and they agree: 0.930
from a tinySA's attenuator cabled in, 0.935 from the same PA field driven
by
.IR tools/radio/paladder.py ,
and 0.949 from this program on the boards.  See
.I doc/radio-plan.ms
items 2 and 7.
.SS Radiocfg
.I Radiocfg
applies the other direction: it reads this board's
.B #P/uid
\- the microcontroller's 96-bit factory serial \- finds the entry in
.B /lib/radio
whose
.B uid
matches, and writes that board's
.BR ppm ,
.B rssioffset
and
.B csthr
to the radio's
.BR ctl .
.PP
.B -n
says what it would write and writes nothing.
.B -s
prints the uid and stops.
.B -f
reads another file and
.B -u
asserts another uid, both for testing somewhere that has neither.
.PP
Every figure is
.B read back
out of the stat file rather than taken from a write that returned, and
every attribute the entry did not have is named.  A calibration silently
not applied looks exactly like one applied wrongly.
.PP
A board with no entry is not an error: it says so and leaves the driver's
defaults, because a new board has no calibration and should still come
up.  A board whose uid cannot be read
.B is
an error, because then it does not know whose numbers it would be using.
.PP
.B rssislope
is reported and not applied.  Nothing in the driver takes it; it is there
so that whoever reads an RSSI knows what a change in it is worth.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/radio
.TP
.B /lib/radio
the calibrations, in the attribute database format of
.IR attrdb (6),
keyed by
.BR uid .
.TP
.B #P/uid
the board's factory serial, twenty-four hex digits.
